From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: aswin-mcw <aswin.kalies@multicorewareinc.com>
Date: Tue, 27 Oct 2026 15:00:00 +0530
Subject: [PATCH 30/31] aarch64: mingw: do not report register copies and loads
 as SEH saves

aarch64_pe_seh_unwind_emit hands every insn up to the end of the
prologue to seh_pattern_emit, not only the frame-related ones, and at
-O2 the scheduler moves ordinary code in among the register saves.  Two
cases of seh_pattern_emit then took that code for a save:

- REG <- REG: "mov xN, sp" into a callee-saved register was emitted via
  seh_emit_save (..., INTVAL (XEXP (src, 1))), but src is the REG
  itself, so XEXP (src, 1) is garbage and cc1 crashed:

    during RTL pass: final
    internal compiler error: Segmentation fault
      seh_pattern_emit  gcc/config/mingw/winnt.cc:1525

  This is "mov x25, sp" taking the address of a local array, e.g. gawk's
  gen-float-table.c or busybox at -O2 -fstack-protector-strong.

- REG <- MEM: a load of a callee-saved register from [sp, #n] was
  emitted as .seh_save_reg.  The assembler accepts it, so the unwind data
  is silently wrong: sed's lib/quotearg.c at -O2 loads a stack-passed
  argument with "ldr x26, [sp, 256]" in the prologue, and an unwind
  through that frame restores the caller's x26 from the argument slot.

Neither a register copy nor a load is a save.  Only the frame pointer
setup (.seh_set_fp) is an unwind operation in the REG case; the MEM case
goes away.

Signed-off-by: aswin-mcw <aswin.kalies@multicorewareinc.com>
---
 gcc/config/mingw/winnt.cc | 24 ++++++++++++------------
 1 file changed, 12 insertions(+), 12 deletions(-)

diff --git a/gcc/config/mingw/winnt.cc b/gcc/config/mingw/winnt.cc
index c5302dd..4e88d54 100644
--- a/gcc/config/mingw/winnt.cc
+++ b/gcc/config/mingw/winnt.cc
@@ -1517,12 +1517,16 @@ seh_pattern_emit (FILE *f, struct seh_frame_state *seh, rtx pat)
 	    switch (GET_CODE (src))
 	      {
 	      case REG:
+		/* Only the frame pointer setup is an unwind operation here.
+		   Every insn up to the end of the prologue is looked at, not
+		   just frame-related ones, and the scheduler can move ordinary
+		   code in among the register saves, e.g. "mov x25, sp" taking
+		   the address of a local array.  That is not a register save
+		   and needs no directive; reading XEXP (src, 1) of the REG
+		   here crashed cc1.  */
 		if (dest == hard_frame_pointer_rtx
 		    && src == stack_pointer_rtx)
 		  fputs ("\t.seh_set_fp\n", f);
-		else if (CALLEE_SAVED_REG_NUMBER (REGNO (dest))
-			 && src == stack_pointer_rtx)
-		  seh_emit_save (f, seh, dest, INTVAL (XEXP (src, 1)));
 		break;
 
 	      case PLUS:
@@ -1548,15 +1552,11 @@ seh_pattern_emit (FILE *f, struct seh_frame_state *seh, rtx pat)
 		  stack_alloc = increment;
 		break;
 
-	      case MEM:
-		src = XEXP (src, 0);
-		if (GET_CODE (src) == PLUS
-		    && GET_CODE (XEXP (src, 0)) == REG
-		    && CALLEE_SAVED_REG_NUMBER (REGNO (dest))
-		    && XEXP (src, 0) == stack_pointer_rtx)
-		  seh_emit_save (f, seh, dest, INTVAL (XEXP (src, 1)));
-		break;
-
+	      /* A load into a register is never a save.  In the prologue it
+		 is ordinary code the scheduler moved in, e.g. a stack-passed
+		 argument loaded into a callee-saved register; reporting it as
+		 a save would make the unwinder restore that register from the
+		 argument slot.  */
 	      default:
 		break;
 	      }
